course: geography
lesson: 62
title: The River Thames and the Sea Rat's voyage: from Constantinople to the English Channel
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The River in «The Wind in the Willows» never gets a name, but it is really the Thames, where Kenneth Grahame grew up and walked all his life: willows, water-meadows, weirs, locks and big houses on the banks.
explain: The Rat has never been further than the ring of Downs he can see to the south, until a travelling Sea Rat sits down by the road and tells him about his voyages round the Mediterranean Sea: Constantinople, the Greek islands, Venice, Sicily, Corsica, Marseilles and Lisbon.
explain: All of those places are real and you can follow the Sea Rat's voyage on a map, port by port, ending in the harbours of Cornwall and Devon and up the English Channel.
explain: The Sea Rat is in «the coasting trade»: his ships hop along the coasts from harbour to harbour, rarely out of sight of land, which is how most trading ships sailed for thousands of years.
explain: So a story about a river bank can also be a geography lesson about the whole of Europe.
fact: The Thames is about 346 km long; it rises in the Cotswold hills near Kemble, flows through Oxford, Reading and Windsor, and reaches the North Sea through London and a wide estuary.
fact: A river starts at its source, is joined by smaller streams called tributaries, and ends at its mouth; where a river meets the sea and the tide comes in, the wide part is called an estuary.
fact: There are 45 locks on the Thames above London, and below Teddington Lock the river is tidal, rising and falling twice a day with the sea.
fact: The Downs, like the ones the Rat looks at, are rounded hills of chalk in southern England, covered in short grass.
fact: Several big houses on the Thames are said to have inspired Toad Hall, but nobody knows for sure which one Grahame had in mind.
fact: Constantinople is the old name of Istanbul in Turkey; the city stands on both sides of the Bosphorus strait, so part of it is in Europe and part in Asia.
fact: Venice in Italy is built on more than a hundred small islands in a lagoon, so it has canals instead of many of its streets, and people travel by boat, including the long black gondolas.
fact: Sicily is the biggest island in the Mediterranean Sea, and Palermo is its main city; Sardinia belongs to Italy and Corsica, just north of it, belongs to France.
fact: Marseilles, in the south of France, was founded by Greek sailors about 2,600 years ago, which makes it one of the oldest cities in France; today it is a busy port.
fact: To sail from the Mediterranean to Lisbon, the capital of Portugal, a ship passes through the Strait of Gibraltar, where Europe and Africa are only about 14 km apart.
fact: The city of Porto, which the Sea Rat calls Oporto, gave its name to the whole country of Portugal; Bordeaux is a port on a river in south-west France.
fact: The Mediterranean has hot, dry summers and mild, wet winters, which suits olive trees, grapes and lemons, so the Sea Rat could rest in «the great olive-woods» on the hills.
fact: «The Mountains of the Moon» was an old name for mountains in Africa thought to be where the Nile began; for the Rat it means the very edge of his known world.
exercise: Which real river is the River in the book? | The Thames | The Severn | The Nile | The Danube
exercise: Where does the Thames begin? | In the Cotswold hills | In the middle of London | In the mountains of Wales | In the North Sea
exercise: What is a tributary? | A smaller stream that joins a river | The wide mouth of a river at the sea | A wall built across a river | A boat that carries goods
exercise: What is an estuary? | The wide part where a river meets the tide | The place high in the hills where a river starts | A lake in the middle of a river | A bridge over a river
exercise: Below which lock is the Thames tidal? | Teddington Lock | Oxford Lock | The lock at the source | No lock at all
exercise: What are the Downs made of? | Chalk | Granite | Sand | Ice
exercise: Which house was Toad Hall? | Nobody knows for sure | Buckingham Palace in London | Windsor Castle on the hill | The Tower of London
exercise: What is the modern name of Constantinople? | Istanbul | Athens | Alexandria | Rome
exercise: Why is Istanbul in two continents? | It stands on both sides of the Bosphorus strait | It was moved from Asia to Europe brick by brick | It is built on a boat that sails between them | Half of it belongs to Italy
exercise: What is Venice built on? | Over a hundred small islands in a lagoon | One big rocky hill high above the sea | The flat top of an old volcano | Hundreds of wooden ships tied together
exercise: Which is the biggest island in the Mediterranean? | Sicily | Corsica | Sardinia | Malta
exercise: Which country does Corsica belong to? | France | Italy | Spain | Greece
exercise: Who founded Marseilles? | Greek sailors | Roman soldiers | Viking raiders | English merchants
exercise: Which strait must a ship pass to go from the Mediterranean to Lisbon? | The Strait of Gibraltar | The Bosphorus near Istanbul | The English Channel | The Strait of Dover by Calais
exercise: Which city gave its name to Portugal? | Porto | Lisbon | Bordeaux | Madrid
exercise: What kind of weather suits olive trees? | Hot dry summers and mild wet winters | Cold snowy winters and cool, cloudy summers | Rain on almost every day of the year | Frost and ice nearly all year round
exercise: What does a ship in the coasting trade do? | Sails from port to port along the coast | Sails straight across the middle of the ocean | Only sails up and down rivers | Never leaves the harbour
test: «the river in wind in the willows is made up, there's no real one» What is right? | It is based on the real River Thames, where Grahame grew up | It is the River Nile in Egypt, with the names changed | It is a river on the Moon that Grahame dreamed about | It is a canal in Manchester full of barges
test: The Rat and Otter explore «the wide uplands» where small streams feed their River. What are those streams? | Tributaries | Estuaries | Weirs and mill-races | Locks and sluices
test: «my nan lives on the thames in london and the water goes up and down every day, is that a flood?» What is the best answer? | No, below Teddington the river is tidal and rises and falls with the sea | Yes, the Thames floods London twice a day, every day of the year | The river is slowly draining away into the sea for good | Someone at Oxford is opening a big tap upstream every morning
test: The Sea Rat says the port he comes from is Constantinople. Where would you find it on a map today? | Istanbul in Turkey, on the Bosphorus | Athens, the capital of Greece | Venice, in the north of Italy | Lisbon, the capital of Portugal
test: «venice has no roads so how do people get about» What is right? | Many travel by boat along the canals, or walk over bridges | Everyone flies about in small helicopters from roof to roof | People swim from island to island every morning | They ride horses through the shallow water
test: The Sea Rat sails from Corsica to the Italian mainland, then on to Marseilles. Which countries is he in? | France, then Italy, then France | Spain, then Greece, then Spain | Italy all the way | Portugal, then France, then Spain
test: A friend says the Sea Rat could sail from Marseilles to Lisbon without leaving the Mediterranean. What is wrong? | Lisbon is on the Atlantic, so he must pass the Strait of Gibraltar | Lisbon is an island in the very middle of the Mediterranean | Lisbon is on a big river in the north of Africa | Nothing is wrong, he can stay in that sea
test: The Sea Rat rests in «the great olive-woods» high above the sea. Why do olives grow well there? | Mediterranean summers are hot and dry and winters mild | It snows there for most of the year, which olives love | It rains every single day, so the trees never dry out | Olives grow only in deep shade under other trees
test: For the Rat, the Downs are «his Mountains of the Moon». What does he mean? | They are the edge of the world he knows | They are made of moon rock | He has climbed them every day | They are the highest mountains on Earth
test: «sea rat goes coasting, is that like coasting on a bike downhill?» What does it mean here? | Sailing from harbour to harbour along the coast | Letting the ship drift along with no sails up at all | Sailing only downhill on rivers to the sea | Sliding a boat along the sandy beach on rollers
test: ★★★ The Sea Rat sails from Marseilles to Lisbon in 9 days, rests 3 days, then sails to Cornwall in 6 days. If he leaves Marseilles on 1 June, on what date does he reach Cornwall? | 19 June | 18 June | 16 June | 21 June
exam: Identify the river upon which the River of the novel is principally based. | The Thames, flowing from the Cotswolds through London to the North Sea | The Severn, flowing from the mountains of Wales to the Bristol Channel | The Rhine | The Seine
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the city formerly designated Constantinople. | It is now Istanbul and is situated on both shores of the Bosphorus | It is now Athens and is situated upon the shores of the Aegean Sea | It lies wholly within Asia | It was abandoned in ancient times
exam: Ascertain the passage through which a vessel must proceed in sailing from Marseilles to Lisbon. | The Strait of Gibraltar | The Bosphorus | The Suez Canal, constructed in 1869 | The Strait of Dover
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the coasting trade in which the Sea Rat is engaged? | Vessels proceed from port to port along the coast, seldom out of sight of land | Vessels cross the open ocean without sight of land for many months together | Vessels navigate inland rivers exclusively | Vessels remain at anchor in a single harbour
